== History ==

The Torre Senador was constructed between 1986 and 1989 as part of the wave of tower development that transformed the Districte Financer during the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]]. At 215 metres, it is shorter than the nearby [[Centre Financer Internacional (Balboa)|Centre Financer Internacional]] (265 metres) and the [[Torre CBNM]] (270 metres). The tower was conceived as a mixed-use project combining premium office space with a luxury hotel, capitalising on the demand for business accommodation and corporate headquarters near the [[Balboa Canal|canal corridor]].

The [[Hotel Senador]], which traces its history to a colonial-era hotel destroyed in the [[Great Adonian War]], was re-established in the tower as its anchor hotel tenant. The hotel occupies floors 18 through 35, with the tower taking its name from the hotel.

== Use ==

The Torre Senador operates as a mixed-use building. The lower floors contain the hotel lobby, retail, restaurants, and conference facilities. The middle floors house the [[Hotel Senador]], a five-star hotel with 312 rooms and suites. The lower-mid and upper floors are occupied by office tenants, including financial services firms, law firms, and corporate offices associated with the Districte Financer.

